---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../site";
---

<Base
  title="Docs — how the AI browser agent works"
  description="Browsergent documentation: agent loop, run_js tool, page.* command protocol, BYOK configuration, skills, limits, and safety for the Chrome AI agent."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Docs", href: "/docs/" }]}
>
  <article class="container container-prose prose">
    <header class="doc-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p>
      <h1>How Browsergent works</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Browsergent is an AI browser agent for Chrome. You give it a task in plain
        English; it reasons with an LLM, generates JavaScript, runs that JS in a
        sandboxed runtime, observes the result, and iterates until the task is
        done.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <strong>On this page</strong>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#core-principle">Core principle</a></li>
        <li><a href="#agent-loop">Agent loop</a></li>
        <li><a href="#command-protocol">Command protocol</a></li>
        <li><a href="#configuration">Configuration</a></li>
        <li><a href="#skills">Skills &amp; files</a></li>
        <li><a href="#limitations">Limitations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#safety">Safety</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <section id="core-principle">
      <h2>The core principle</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>LLM does reasoning, JS does acting.</strong> The model&rsquo;s
        only browser tool is <code>run_js</code>. It never calls Chrome APIs or
        touches the DOM directly &mdash; it writes JavaScript, and the
        <code>@pi-oxide/extension-js</code> runtime turns that into typed
        <code>page.*</code> commands executed against the active tab.
      </p>
      <p>
        The brain is a Rust state machine compiled to WASM
        (<code>@pi-oxide/pi-host-web</code>) running in a Web Worker. The LLM is
        called over the Anthropic Messages API. Side effects happen only through
        the typed command protocol.
      </p>
      <p>
        That separation is the product thesis: an open-source browser agent can
        stay powerful without giving the model raw <code>chrome.*</code> access.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="agent-loop">
      <h2>The agent loop</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Observe</strong> — snapshot the active tab into a structured element list (refs, roles, labels).</li>
        <li><strong>Reason</strong> — send the task + history + snapshot to the LLM.</li>
        <li><strong>Act</strong> — the model returns JavaScript via <code>run_js</code>; the runtime dispatches <code>page.*</code> commands.</li>
        <li><strong>Observe again</strong> — the result feeds back into the next turn.</li>
        <li><strong>Stop</strong> — the model signals completion or you stop the run.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If you have used Claude Code, the rhythm will feel familiar: plan, tool
        call, observation, repeat. Browsergent applies that loop to a live
        Chrome tab instead of a local filesystem.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="command-protocol">
      <h2>The command protocol</h2>
      <p>
        Every browser action is a typed <code>BrowserCommand</code>. A few
        examples:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`type BrowserCommand =
  | { kind: "page.snapshot"; options?: SnapshotOptions }
  | { kind: "page.click";   refId: RefId }
  | { kind: "page.fill";    refId: RefId; text: string }
  | { kind: "page.select";  refId: RefId; value: string }
  | { kind: "page.scroll";  direction: "up" | "down" }
  | { kind: "page.goto";    url: string }
  | { kind: "page.wait";    ms: number };`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Results are <code>BrowserResult</code> &mdash; an <code>ok</code> value
        or a typed error with a machine-readable <code>code</code> (e.g.
        <code>E_STALE</code>, <code>E_NOT_FOUND</code>). The model sees errors
        and recovers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="configuration">
      <h2>Configuration</h2>
      <p>Open <strong>Settings</strong> in the side panel and provide:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>API Key</td><td><code>sk-ant-api03-…</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Base URL</td><td><code>https://api.anthropic.com</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Model</td><td><code>claude-sonnet-4-6</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Compatible providers: <strong>Anthropic</strong>,
        <strong>DeepSeek</strong> (<code>api.deepseek.com/anthropic</code>),
        <strong>z.ai / GLM</strong>, or any endpoint implementing the Anthropic
        Messages API. Your key stays in the browser &mdash; it&rsquo;s only ever
        sent to the base URL you configure.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prefer the <a href="/download/">prebuilt Chrome extension zip</a> if you
        just want to try the agent. Build from source when you want to inspect or
        modify the runtime.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="skills">
      <h2>Skills &amp; files</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><code>/skill:&lt;name&gt;</code> activates a reusable skill at compose time &mdash; its instructions are folded into the prompt.</li>
        <li><code>@[file:name.ext]</code> attaches a session file (stored in OPFS) into the task.</li>
        <li>The <strong>Files</strong> panel lets you upload, edit, and manage session files.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="limitations">
      <h2>Known limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Chrome only.</strong> MV3 side panel + content scripts; not ported to Firefox/Safari.</li>
        <li><strong>Anthropic Messages API only.</strong> OpenAI-native function-calling isn&rsquo;t supported on the wire.</li>
        <li><strong>Drives your real tab</strong> (not a remote browser farm).</li>
        <li><strong>Closing the side panel stops agent runs</strong> (by design). While the panel stays open, other sessions may keep running in the background of that panel (not on the chat frontend).</li>
        <li><strong>Context-bound.</strong> Long sessions are compacted, but very long tasks may lose earlier detail.</li>
        <li><strong>Single tab.</strong> The agent operates on one active tab at a time.</li>
        <li><strong>Stale refIds.</strong> Snapshot refs (<code>eNNN</code>) are single-use within an observation &mdash; reusing one is the most common failure.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="safety">
      <h2>Experimental &amp; safety</h2>
      <p>
        Browsergent is <strong>experimental</strong>. Its current
        philosophy is to expose everything the Chrome extension can access so we
        can learn the boundary of browser-agent capability. It may read page
        content, cookies, auth headers, and request/response metadata. Always
        review its actions and avoid using it on accounts where that level of
        access is unacceptable.
      </p>
      <p>
        More context: <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> · <a href="/use-cases/">use cases</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Going deeper</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href={SITE.repo}>Source on GitHub</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`}>Architecture (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md)</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/AGENTS.md`}>Contributor conventions (AGENTS.md)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/download/">Download the latest release →</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .doc-head { margin: 3.5rem 0 2.5rem; }
  .doc-head h1 { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .prose section { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .toc {
    background: var(--bg-subtle);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .toc strong { display: block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .toc ol { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .toc li { margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }
</style>
